## 3. Demand Prediction and Machine Learning

Demand prediction estimates future sales or demand using historical data and relevant market features.

### Essential Concepts

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| Demand Forecasting | Predicting future demand. |
| Feature | An input variable used by a machine learning model. |
| Target Variable | The outcome the model predicts, such as sales quantity. |
| Training Data | Data used to learn model parameters. |
| Validation Data | Data used to compare models and tune hyperparameters. |
| Test Data | Held-out data used for final evaluation. |
| Regression | Predicting numerical values. |
| Classification | Predicting categories. |
| Point Prediction | Producing a single predicted value. |
| Probabilistic Prediction | Representing uncertainty about possible outcomes. |
| Prediction Interval | An interval intended to contain an unknown outcome at a specified coverage level. |
| Calibration | Assessing or adjusting whether predictions and their uncertainty match observed outcomes. |
| Overfitting | Learning training data too closely and performing poorly on unseen data. |
| Generalization | Performing well on unseen data. |
| Feature Engineering | Creating useful features from raw data. |
| Data Leakage | Using information during training that would not be available at decision time. |
| Distribution Shift | A change in the data distribution between training and deployment. |
| Concept Drift | A change in the relationship between inputs and outcomes over time. |

### Important Demand Prediction Models

- Linear Regression
- Random Forest Regressor
- Gradient Boosting
- LightGBM
- XGBoost
- Temporal Fusion Transformer (TFT)
- N-HiTS
- Other probabilistic forecasting models

Model selection should depend on the dataset, forecasting horizon, computational budget, and research objectives.

