## 5. Conformal Prediction and Calibration

Conformal prediction is a framework for constructing prediction sets or intervals with statistical coverage guarantees under appropriate assumptions.

In this research, conformal methods may help quantify uncertainty or regulate how aggressively a new pricing policy is permitted to act.

### Essential Concepts

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| Conformal Prediction | A framework for constructing prediction sets or intervals with coverage guarantees under specified assumptions. |
| Conformal Calibration | Using calibration data to determine prediction or decision thresholds. |
| Calibration Dataset | Data used to calculate calibration quantities. |
| Coverage | The proportion or probability of outcomes contained within a prediction set or interval. |
| Miscoverage | The event that an outcome falls outside the prediction set. |
| Prediction Set | A set of possible outcomes produced by a prediction method. |
| Quantile | A value below which a specified proportion of observations falls. |
| Quantile Regression | Predicting a selected quantile of an outcome distribution. |
| Conformalized Quantile Regression (CQR) | A conformal method that adjusts quantile-based prediction intervals. |
| Adaptive Conformal Prediction | Methods that adapt calibration based on new observations or changing conditions. |
| Risk-Controlled Prediction | Constructing predictions or decisions to control a specified risk measure. |
| Finite-Sample Guarantee | A theoretical guarantee that applies with finite data under stated assumptions. |
| Distribution-Free Guarantee | A guarantee that does not require specifying a particular parametric distribution, although other assumptions may remain necessary. |
| Uncertainty Quantification | Representing uncertainty in predictions or decisions. |

**Important:** Prediction-interval coverage does not automatically guarantee pricing-policy safety. The specific method must establish the required policy-level risk guarantee.

