## 6. Safe Exploration and Risk-Aware Optimization

Safe exploration aims to improve an agent's performance while respecting defined safety requirements.

### Important Concepts

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| Safe Exploration | Learning through new actions while satisfying safety requirements. |
| Reference Policy | An existing policy used as a baseline or safety reference. |
| Candidate Policy | A new policy being evaluated or deployed. |
| Policy Optimization | Improving the strategy used to select actions. |
| Policy Shift | A change in the behavior of a policy. |
| Risk Tolerance | The maximum acceptable risk under a defined risk measure. |
| Safety Constraint | A condition that decisions or outcomes must satisfy. |
| Constraint Violation | Failure to satisfy a specified constraint. |
| Risk Bound | An upper or lower bound on a defined risk measure. |
| Expected Loss | The average value of a defined loss measure. |
| Downside Risk | Risk associated with particularly poor outcomes. |
| Value at Risk (VaR) | A specified quantile of a loss distribution. |
| Conditional Value at Risk (CVaR) | A measure of tail loss beyond a specified VaR threshold, under a chosen convention. |
| Lower Confidence Bound (LCB) | A conservative lower bound on an estimated quantity. |
| Chance Constraint | A constraint requiring a condition to hold with a specified probability. |
| Robust Optimization | Optimizing decisions under uncertainty about the environment or model. |
| Risk Budget | An allocated amount of acceptable risk. |

### Possible Pricing Safety Constraints

- Maintain a minimum profit margin.
- Restrict the maximum price change per decision.
- Avoid prices outside an approved range.
- Respect inventory limitations.
- Control the probability or expected severity of a defined loss.
- Limit violations of specified business constraints.

The constraints should be selected according to the business setting and the formal guarantees the proposed method can support.

