## 7. Optimization and Causal Inference

Prediction alone cannot determine the causal effect of changing a price.

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| Objective Function | The quantity being optimized, such as profit. |
| Constrained Optimization | Optimizing an objective while satisfying restrictions. |
| Optimal Price | The best price under a defined objective, context, and assumptions. |
| Counterfactual | An outcome that would have occurred under an alternative action. |
| Causal Inference | Estimating cause-and-effect relationships. |
| Treatment Effect | The effect of a price change or intervention on an outcome. |
| Confounding | A situation where another factor influences both the action and the outcome. |
| Price Endogeneity | The problem that price may be related to unobserved factors that also influence demand. |
| Double Machine Learning | A framework for estimating certain causal effects using machine learning and appropriate assumptions. |
| Off-Policy Evaluation (OPE) | Estimating the performance of a new policy using data collected by another policy. |
| Inverse Propensity Scoring (IPS) | Reweighting observed outcomes using action-selection probabilities. |
| Doubly Robust Estimation | Combining an outcome model with propensity weighting for policy or causal estimation under suitable assumptions. |
| Simulator | An environment for evaluating pricing decisions without immediately deploying them in a live market. |
| Sensitivity Analysis | Examining how conclusions change under different assumptions. |

Historical transaction data may not reveal demand at prices that were never offered. Counterfactual evaluation therefore requires appropriate assumptions, models, logged action probabilities, randomized variation, or a validated simulator.

