## 8. Evaluation Metrics

A pricing system should be evaluated on both predictive performance and decision-making performance.

### Demand Prediction Metrics

| Metric | Purpose |
|---|---|
| MAE | Measures average absolute prediction error. |
| RMSE | Penalizes larger prediction errors more strongly. |
| Empirical Coverage | Measures how often prediction intervals contain observed outcomes. |
| Interval Width | Measures the width of prediction intervals. |
| Calibration Error | Measures discrepancies between predicted confidence or probabilities and observed outcomes, according to the chosen definition. |

### Pricing Policy Metrics

| Metric | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Revenue | Measures total sales income. |
| Profit | Measures earnings after relevant costs. |
| Cumulative Regret | Measures the cumulative gap from a defined optimal benchmark. |
| Stockout Rate | Measures how frequently stockouts occur. |
| Inventory Leftover | Measures unsold inventory remaining at the end of a period. |
| Constraint Violation Rate | Measures the frequency of safety or business constraint violations. |
| CVaR | Measures tail risk under a defined loss convention. |
| Price Stability | Measures the frequency or magnitude of price changes. |
| Policy Improvement | Measures performance relative to a baseline policy. |
| Computational Cost | Measures the resources required for training and inference. |

Prediction metrics and policy metrics should not be treated as interchangeable. A model with low demand prediction error does not necessarily produce the most profitable pricing policy.

