## 9. Datasets and Experimental Design

### Important Data Terms

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| Transaction Data | Records of completed sales. |
| Historical Price | A price observed in a previous period. |
| Sales Quantity | The number of units sold. |
| Timestamp | The time at which an observation occurred. |
| Product ID | An identifier for a product. |
| Exogenous Variable | An external factor that may influence demand. |
| Time-Series Data | Data organized by time. |
| Temporal Validation | Evaluating a model on later periods than those used for training. |
| Train/Validation/Test Split | Separating data for learning, model selection, and final evaluation. |
| Logged Policy | The policy that generated historical actions. |
| Propensity Score | The probability of an action being selected given the observed context. |
| Action Logging | Recording contexts, actions, outcomes, and relevant selection probabilities. |
| Offline Evaluation | Evaluating a policy using existing data. |
| Online Evaluation | Evaluating a policy in a live environment. |
| A/B Testing | Comparing alternatives through a controlled experiment. |
| Synthetic Data | Artificially generated observations. |
| Ablation Study | Removing components to measure their contribution. |
| Baseline | A reference method used for comparison. |

### Dataset Selection Considerations

A useful dataset should ideally include:

- Historical product prices.
- Sales quantities or demand observations.
- Product identifiers and timestamps.
- Inventory information, when available.
- Promotions and seasonal variables.
- Competitor prices, if relevant to the research question.
- Sufficient variation in prices to evaluate alternative decisions.
- Action-selection probabilities or experimental information, if required for off-policy evaluation.

Not every retail dataset contains all these variables. Missing variables should be documented rather than assumed to exist.

